# Peugeot Type 69 "Bébé", 1905-1912.
#
# The first mass-market small Peugeot, and one of the cars that established the
# light car in Europe: a 652 cc single-cylinder two-seater on a short chassis,
# sold as the "Baby Peugeot" in Britain at under GBP 200 in the 1906 price lists.
# About 400 of the Type 69 were built, with the Type 69 Bébé and its successors
# running to 1912.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peugeot_Type_69
#
# No works dimensions have been found. The model was built to these ESTIMATES:
# about 2.700 m long, 1.300 m wide, 1.500 m high, 1.750 m wheelbase. Correct them
# if a source is found.
#
# Colours: Red, Dark Green, Cream and Black were period finishes; the museum car
# in the references is red with yellow wheels.
#
# Weight: 8 in total (red 3, green 2, cream 2, black 1). The Bébé was the cheap
# end of the Edwardian market, so its parc weight is well below the big cars of
# the same years.
#
# Speed 40 km/h; comfort 25/368 (line). Class 2.
# Dates: 1905-1912 with a road tail to about 1932.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/peugeot-type-69-bebe.blend. References:
# citycars/references/peugeot-type-69-bebe/ (gitignored). Images via Wikimedia
# Commons.

obj=citycar
